    908	measurement on N = π(2·10⁸) ≈ 1.1·10⁷ primes refines E1.3 with
    909	three new structural facts:
    910	- **bit_0(p(n)) is trivially polylog** (= 1 for n ≥ 2). E1.3's
    911	  R⁻¹-agreement metric mis-classifies it as "hard" (ag = 0.500)
    912	  because Li⁻¹'s LSB is uniform mod 2, but the bit IS deterministic.
    913	- **RH-scale anti-correlation valley**: ag_Li(J) drops to ~0.36
    914	  at J* = ⌊log₂(p(N))/2⌋ — the bit-level signature of Skewes-
    915	  direction error sign in `Li(x) > π(x)`. Position shifts with
    916	  N as predicted; magnitude stable across N ∈ {10⁷, 5·10⁷, 2·10⁸}.
    917	- **PNT first-order ≠ Li⁻¹ at bit level**: top-bit ag_PNT < 0.95
    918	  while ag_Li → 1.0; full PNT closed form is required.
    919	The original F1 question (largest J for provable polylog) remains
